Dynamic languages suffer from ambiguity. Consider two-to-one mapping:

```
pushViewController(message: String, title: String, style: AlertStyle = Default) -> Boolean
pushViewController(title: String, style: AlertStyle = Default, message: String = "") -> Boolean
```

Because you can't tell from the name whether the method is given a title parameter, or a message parameter, and an optional style parameter, the compiler can't decide which implementation to call. This results in ambiguous error messages like warning 10038:
"A method at Nov 21'21 3:16:53 AM/9/Button.escape() is 'implicit ambiguous' because of signature mismatch. Did you mean to override that method instead of implementing it here?. It should be either Default() or Bind()".

This ambiguity is easy to rectify by changing the signature, so for example:

```
override func goToView(message: String, title: String, style: AlertStyle = Default) -> Void {
    handleGoToResult(success: false, message: nil)
    if let url = URL(string: "#/w/login") {
        let path = localize(" Login", "Login")
        AppleEventNS.mkURL(makeNsCString: path,
            arg0: "Login", arg1: url,
            options: AEKNO_COPYPATH | AEKNO_EXTRACTPATH)
            .getAbsoluteURL()
            .asFunction(arguments: (pushCount, title, true) as [Any?]).executeNative(external: true)
    }
}
```

Now, the compiler can't determine ambiguity is there's only one implementation of the PushView method that accepts any number of message, title and style parameters, but there is only one implementation of GoToView method that accepts any number of message, title and style parameters, there is no ambiguity between the method implementations.

